				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-04-15T13:40:06.579641-04:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "A/3gF9JzSvife3CpWIlayw=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "# Findings (2026-04-15)\n\nThe plan above was written from research; what follows is grounded in delivered, committed code.\n\n## ZIP64 is real, and it's a blocker\n\nPharo's {{gtClass:ZipArchive}} cannot seek into ZIP64 archives. Confirmed empirically: npm's `all.zip` today has **217,363 entries** and uses ZIP64 sentinels (`0xFFFFFFFF`) in the classic End-of-Central-Directory record. `ZipArchive>>#readEndOfCentralDirectoryFrom:` reads those fields as 32-bit unsigned ints and then computes `stream position: eocdPosition - centralDirectorySize`, which with size=4294967295 goes negative — the `setPosition:to:` primitive refuses it. Image grep: zero methods/classes containing \"64\" in the archive machinery. We decided *not* to upstream-patch Pharo ZipArchive; the subprocess wrapper is cleaner for our scope.\n\n## Native unzip is the right answer, and it was painless\n\n{{gtClass:Gt4SecurityUnzipTool}} wraps `/usr/bin/unzip` (Info-ZIP v6, ZIP64-aware) via {{gtClass:GtSubprocessWithInMemoryOutput}} — the same infrastructure used by {{gtClass:GtAwsClient}}, {{gtClass:GtLSPRustModel}}, etc. Zip Slip is structurally neutralized on single-entry reads because `unzip -p` writes to stdout (our choice of destination) instead of the filesystem; entry-name allowlist regex is only defense-in-depth. Single-entry extract measures ~113 ms; listing all entries on the 192 MB npm zip takes ~3 s. Bulk extract via `unzip -o -d` works but is slow-by-design (210K small file writes + reads) — we don't use it as a primary path.\n\n## GT has a full async framework — use it\n\n{{gtClass:AsyncJoinAllFuture}} + {{gtClass:AsyncFutureExecutionConfiguration}} with `customGroup:` and `maxAmountOfWorkers:` gives us bounded concurrency without homebuilt pools. We validated the pattern with a 20-fetch-cap-4 test (534 ms elapsed, ~4.2x speedup over 2260 ms serial — the cap held exactly). The ergonomics are clean: wrap a block in `asAsyncFuture`, schedule via `await: config`, collect results with `wait`. Error model: default `AsyncNoneErrorHandler` stores rejections as the promise's value rather than re-raising — caller-side `isKindOf: Error` check per result. We retired {{gtMethod:Gt4SecurityOsvFeedClient>>#fetchVulnsFromRows:concurrency:}}'s SharedQueue + Mutex + manual fork plumbing. Same parity on npm 7-day fetch (323 vulns, 152 malicious, 171 GHSA) but faster (9s vs 15s — pool reuse).\n\n## Final architecture (committed)\n\nOSV bulk feed is the canonical path for ecosystem-wide queries; the REST API is per-package and can't answer \"all npm in last 90 days\". Within the bulk feed we split by query shape:\n\n- **Date-bounded** (`recentModifiedSince:ecosystem:concurrency:`) → filter `modified_id.csv` for matching rows, fan out individual JSON fetches in parallel via async futures with concurrency 16. Works on every ecosystem including ZIP64.\n- **Full dump** (`ecosystemFull:`) → try Pharo {{gtClass:ZipArchive}} first (fast, in-memory); fall back to `Gt4SecurityUnzipTool extractAllTo:from:` on error (handles ZIP64). Tmpdir per call, `ensure:` cleanup.\n- **Single entry from cached zip** — primitive `Gt4SecurityUnzipTool contentsOfEntry:in:` — structurally safe, ~113 ms, no HTTP.\n\nAll wired into {{gtClass:Gt4SecurityQuery}} via `feedEcosystem:` — when `publishedSince:` is set, routes through the date-bounded path; otherwise the full-dump path."
			},
